# Cloud Computing Concepts Reflection

Cloud computing has become a part of my everyday activities, even though I do not always notice that I am using it. Many applications and services that I use today rely on cloud technology to store data, provide online services, and allow users to access information anytime and anywhere.

One cloud service that I frequently use is **Google Drive**. I use it for storing files, documents, and school-related activities. Google Drive is an example of **Software as a Service (SaaS)** because it provides ready-to-use applications through the internet without requiring users to manage servers or hardware. It uses the **Public Cloud** deployment model because anyone with an account can access the service online.

Another cloud-based application I use is **Facebook Messenger**. It is also considered **SaaS** because it provides communication services such as messaging, voice calls, and video calls through an online platform. It uses the **Public Cloud** model because the service is available to millions of users around the world.

The third service I use is **GCash**, a digital payment application that allows me to send money, pay bills, and perform other financial transactions. GCash can be classified as **SaaS** because it provides financial services through a mobile application. It can be considered a **Hybrid Cloud** because it combines cloud services with private and secured systems to protect users' financial information.

Git and GitHub are also important tools when working with cloud computing projects. Version control helps developers keep track of changes, avoid losing important files, and return to previous versions when problems occur. GitHub allows teams to collaborate by sharing code, reviewing updates, and managing project changes in an organized way.

Learning cloud computing and version control helps me understand how modern applications are developed and maintained. These skills are important because technology today depends on teamwork, secure data management, and reliable systems.
